@page "/"
@layout MainLayout
@inject DemoDbService Db

<PageTitle>BlazeDb - a memory-first database for Blazor</PageTitle>

@* The landing page: hero → proof strip → capability grid → code walkthrough → comparison with
   sqlite-wasm → persona tiles → FAQ → final call to action. Everything else on the site is a
   documentation page under DocsLayout. The hero's sample is this tab's real database rather than a
   picture of one: the row it inserts is a row on every other page too. *@

<div class="bb-landing">

    @* ============================== Hero ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-hero">
        <div class="bb-hero-copy">
            <span class="bb-hero-eyebrow">
                <span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span>
                Open source · MIT · net8.0 / net9.0 / net10.0
            </span>
            <h1>Your data lives in memory.<br /><span class="bb-hero-accent">Durability is a background job.</span></h1>
            <p class="bb-hero-sub">
                BlazeDb is a SQL-free embedded database for .NET and Blazor WebAssembly that keeps every row as a
                strongly typed object in managed memory. Reads are plain method calls into hash and ordered indexes -
                no query parsing, no JavaScript interop, no I/O. Writes commit to memory and stream to a write-ahead
                log that reaches OPFS in the background.
            </p>
            <pre class="bb-code bb-hero-install">dotnet add package Bit.BlazeDb</pre>
            <div class="bb-hero-actions">
                <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-primary bb-btn-lg">Get started →</a>
                <a href="todos" class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg">Open the live app</a>
                <a class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg" href="@SiteInfo.ProjectUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* The sample, framed as a window: the calls on top, and what they return underneath - against
           the database this tab opened when the app started. *@
        <div class="bb-hero-code">
            <div class="bb-browser">
                <div class="bb-browser-bar">
                    <span class="bb-browser-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
                    <span class="bb-browser-url">Todos.razor · <strong>@(Db.IsOpen ? Db.StorageLabel : "opening…")</strong></span>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">var</span> todos = db.GetTable(TodoEntry.Table);

todos.Insert(<span class="k">new</span> TodoEntry
{
    Id = Guid.NewGuid(), Title = title, CreatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow,
});

<span class="k">var</span> open = todos.CountBy(TodoEntry.Indexes.Done, <span class="k">false</span>); <span class="c">// a bucket size</span>
<span class="k">var</span> newest = todos.OrderBy(TodoEntry.Indexes.CreatedAt, descending: <span class="k">true</span>);</pre>
                <div class="bd-hero-live">
                    @if (_error is not null)
                    {
                        <p class="bd-err">@_error</p>
                    }
                    else if (!Db.IsOpen)
                    {
                        <p class="bb-muted"><span class="bb-spinner"></span> Opening this tab's database…</p>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <div class="bd-hero-add">
                            <input id="hero-title" aria-label="Title of a new todo" placeholder="Add a todo to this tab's database"
                                   @bind="_title" @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="OnKey" />
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" disabled="@string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_title)" @onclick="Insert">Insert</button>
                        </div>
                        <dl class="bd-hero-stats">
                            <div><dt>todos.Count</dt><dd>@Db.Todos.Count.ToString("N0")</dd></div>
                            <div><dt>open</dt><dd>@Db.Todos.CountBy(TodoEntry.Indexes.Done, false).ToString("N0")</dd></div>
                            <div><dt>last Insert</dt><dd>@_lastInsert</dd></div>
                        </dl>
                        <ol class="bd-hero-rows" aria-label="Newest todos">
                            @foreach (var row in Db.Todos.OrderBy(TodoEntry.Indexes.CreatedAt, descending: true).Take(3))
                            {
                                <li @key="row.Id"><span>@row.Title</span><span class="bb-mono">@row.CreatedAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss")</span></li>
                            }
                        </ol>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Proof strip ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-proof">
        <div><strong>1.2 ns point reads</strong><span>a dictionary probe, zero allocations, no statement to bind</span></div>
        <div><strong>No SQL, no interop</strong><span>typed <code>Get</code>, <code>Lookup</code>, <code>Range</code> and <code>Scan</code> on the read path</span></div>
        <div><strong>WAL + snapshots</strong><span>atomic commits, crash recovery, persisted to OPFS</span></div>
        <div><strong>Zero runtime reflection</strong><span>a source generator emits the schema - trimming and AOT safe</span></div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Capability grid ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Capabilities</p>
        <h2>A database that is just objects and indexes</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            Rows are the objects your code already uses, and every query is a call into an index you declared - the
            engine around them adds atomic transactions, durability and an EF Core provider, and nothing you would have
            to parse.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-grid">
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Schema from your types</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>[BlazeDbTable]</code>, <code>[BlazeDbKey]</code> and the index attributes on a partial class -
                    the generator emits the serializers, the key extractor and a typed handle for every index.
                </p>
                <a href="schema" class="btn btn-outline">Schema &amp; codegen →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Hash and ordered indexes</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Equality in O(1), ranges and sort order in O(log n), compound and unique indexes - maintained on
                    every insert, update, delete and rollback.
                </p>
                <a href="indexes" class="btn btn-outline">Indexes →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Queries without a language</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>BlazeDbQuery</code> composes one index source, residual predicates, ordering and paging - and
                    <code>ExecuteAsync</code> streams a large result without freezing the browser's only thread.
                </p>
                <a href="queries" class="btn btn-outline">Query primitives →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> An EF Core provider</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>AddDbContext</code> plus <code>UseBlazeDb</code>: LINQ is translated onto the same index plan,
                    and <code>SaveChanges</code> is one engine transaction over the live rows.
                </p>
                <a href="efcore" class="btn btn-outline">EF Core →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Crash-safe durability</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Group-committed log records with a CRC each, snapshot checkpoints and an atomic manifest - a torn
                    tail is truncated on replay, real corruption is reported rather than hidden.
                </p>
                <a href="durability" class="btn btn-outline">Durability &amp; recovery →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Built for the browser</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    OPFS and IndexedDB backends, a Web Lock so one tab writes, <a href="tabs">read-only replica tabs</a>,
                    quota awareness and <a href="encryption">encryption at rest</a> through WebCrypto.
                </p>
                <a href="storage" class="btn btn-outline">Storage backends →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Reactive by default</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Every table reports the net change of each commit, and a live query re-runs only when a commit
                    concerns it - so a component re-renders when its data changed, and only then.
                </p>
                <a href="changes" class="btn btn-outline">Changes &amp; live queries →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Backups and migrations</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    One checksummed export file that imports onto any backend, a <code>UserVersion</code> stored with the
                    data, and migrations that bring a database forward when it opens.
                </p>
                <a href="backups" class="btn btn-outline">Backups &amp; migrations →</a>
            </section>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Code walkthrough ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">In practice</p>
        <h2>See it in code</h2>
        <div class="bb-walkthrough">
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">01</span>
                    <h3>Declare a table</h3>
                    <p>
                        A partial class with one key and the indexes you query by. The generator turns it into
                        <code>TodoItem.Table</code> and <code>TodoItem.Indexes</code> at compile time.
                    </p>
                    <a href="schema">Schema &amp; codegen →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">[<span class="p">BlazeDbTable</span>(<span class="s">"todos"</span>)]
<span class="k">public partial class</span> TodoItem
{
    [<span class="p">BlazeDbKey</span>] <span class="k">public</span> Guid Id { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; }
    <span class="k">public string</span> Title { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; } = <span class="s">""</span>;
    [<span class="p">BlazeDbIndex</span>] <span class="k">public bool</span> Done { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; }
}</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">02</span>
                    <h3>Open it over OPFS</h3>
                    <p>
                        The storage is the only thing that says where the bytes go - the origin private file system in
                        the browser, a directory on the desktop, a dictionary in a test.
                    </p>
                    <a href="storage">Storage backends →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">var</span> options = <span class="k">new</span> BlazeDbDatabaseOptions
{
    Storage = <span class="k">await</span> BlazeDbOpfsStorage.CreateAsync(<span class="s">"mydb"</span>),
}.AddTable(TodoItem.Table);

<span class="k">await using var</span> db = <span class="k">await</span> BlazeDbDatabase.OpenAsync(options);</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">03</span>
                    <h3>Read from memory, write in batches</h3>
                    <p>
                        A lookup is an index probe with no I/O. A transaction makes a batch of writes one atomic log
                        record, flushed in the background - or now, with <code>FlushAsync</code>.
                    </p>
                    <a href="transactions">Transactions →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">var</span> todos = db.GetTable(TodoItem.Table);
<span class="k">var</span> open = todos.Lookup(TodoItem.Indexes.Done, <span class="k">false</span>); <span class="c">// no I/O, no SQL, no interop</span>

<span class="k">using</span> (<span class="k">var</span> txn = db.BeginTransaction())
{
    todos.Insert(<span class="k">new</span> TodoItem { Id = Guid.NewGuid(), Title = <span class="s">"hello"</span> });
    txn.Commit();
}</pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Why not sqlite-wasm or an IndexedDB wrapper?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            Client-side databases for Blazor pay on every operation: JavaScript interop marshalling, worker round-trips
            and SQL parsing. BlazeDb removes all three. BenchmarkDotNet on .NET 10, a 10,000-row table, against native
            in-memory SQLite with prepared statements - a favourable stand-in for sqlite-wasm, which pays the interop on
            top.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="BlazeDb compared with SQLite">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Operation</th><th>SQLite</th><th>BlazeDb</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Point get by key</td><td>743 ns</td><td>1.2 ns, zero allocations</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Count by hash index</td><td>63,020 ns - a table scan</td><td>2.1 ns - a bucket size</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ordered range scan (~5,100 rows)</td><td>69.2 µs</td><td>45.4 µs - both index-driven</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Upsert, autocommit</td><td>706 ns</td><td>413 ns with the WAL, 311 ns memory-only</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Upsert in a 1,000-op transaction</td><td>620 ns per op</td><td>369 ns per op, journaled</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Query language</td><td>SQL, parsed and planned</td><td>Typed calls into your indexes, or LINQ through EF Core</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="bb-try">
            <a href="bench" class="btn btn-outline">Measure it in this browser →</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Persona CTAs ============================== *@

