    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is the whole database held in memory?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes, by design. Everything is loaded on open and every read is served from managed memory, which is
                    what keeps I/O off the read path. That is the right trade for a client-side application working with
                    its own user's data, and the wrong one for a shared multi-tenant database.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>What happens to my data if the tab closes mid-write?</summary>
                <p>
                    Commits reach the write-ahead log within the flush interval - 100 ms by default - and
                    <code>FlushAsync</code> closes that window to zero. On the next open the log is replayed up to the last
                    record whose checksum verifies; a torn tail is truncated, and a corrupt manifest or snapshot throws
                    <code>BlazeDbCorruptDatabaseException</code> instead of silently losing data.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>What if the app is open in two tabs?</summary>
                <p>
                    One tab wins a Web Lock and writes; <code>CreateAsync</code> throws
                    <code>BlazeDbDatabaseLockedException</code> in the others, which can open the same database read-only
                    and reload whenever the writer checkpoints - <a href="tabs">try it across two tabs</a>. This site's own
                    database falls back to memory instead: open it in a second tab and the storage badge says so.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it run outside the browser?</summary>
                <p>
                    The engine has no browser dependency: on Blazor Server, ASP.NET Core, MAUI, WPF or a console tool it
                    persists through <code>BlazeDbFileStorage</code>. It assumes one logical writer with no concurrent
                    readers, so a multi-threaded server has to serialize access itself.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can the data on disk be encrypted?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. <code>BlazeDbEncryptedStorage</code> wraps any backend and seals every write into an AES-GCM frame -
                    with WebCrypto in the browser, where the managed <code>AesGcm</code> is unavailable, and a key derived
                    from a passphrase with PBKDF2. A wrong key or a changed byte is reported as corruption, never decoded.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>How do I change the shape of my data later?</summary>
                <p>
                    Adding or removing a property needs nothing: rows are tagged by field number, unknown fields are
                    skipped and missing ones read as defaults - pin the numbers with <code>[BlazeDbField]</code> before you
                    ship. For changes to the data itself, <code>AddMigration(version, …)</code> runs each step once, in a
                    transaction of its own, when the database opens; <code>ExportAsync</code> keeps a backup first.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Is it trimming and AOT safe?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. The serializers, the key extractor and the index definitions are generated at compile time, so
                    nothing is reflected at runtime and the WebAssembly linker can trim everything unused.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

